Content-Type: text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit
From: Sascha Ziemann <szi@aibon.ping.de>
Date: 15 Dec 1998 18:21:38 +0100
Message-ID: <7upv9lfhi5.fsf@olivia.aibon.ping.de>
Lines: 47
X-Mailer: Gnus v5.5/Emacs 20.2

Hi,

I installed Redhat 5.2 on a Noname board (AXPpci33).  The system works
as long as I do not trie to use an Ethernet card.  I tried a
EtherPower II (epic100) and an Acton Combo (tulip).  With both cards I
have the same problem: I can load the driver via modprobe or insmod,
but when I call ifconfig the first time, the system hangs.  No
warning, no error, no panic.  The system stops only and I have to do a
hard reset.

I use the 2.0.35 kernel that comes with Redhat 5.2 (I have no idea why
they did not put 2.0.36 on the CD) with the original drivers and a
brand new epic100 driver.  It is allways the same with both cards.

The system prints for every simple program call six times
"unimplemented OSF mmap flags 0402".  That is quite annoing but till
now I thought that those messages do not matter. But now I am not sure
any longer.

The next problem is that I get quite strange unresolved symbol errors,
when I run "depmod -a". There are even in nfs.o unresolved symbols
although I have no idea on what the nfs code should depend on.
Networking support is of course part of the kernel.  And the system
warns most penetrating that it can not load the modules for IPX and
Appletalk, although I absolutely do not whant that bullshit in my
kernel.

The whole system is a plain vanilla Redhat 5.2.  I used for nearly
every installation option the default value.  I replaced only the
epic100 driver, because I was 0.99 and now it's 1.04.  Does anybody
know what can cause so much trouble with a really simpel default
installation?

The system has a NCR810 with a SCSI disk and CD-Rom, 32MB RAM, 256K
Cache and a ET4000-W32 from Hercules.  I installed also a ISA NE2000
compatible card and an ISA Teles ISDN card.  Both cards need a IRQ and
IO-Ports.  On a Intel system I would tell the Bios that those IRQs
should be ignored by the BIOS when it gives the IRQs to the PCI cards.
Is it possible that the above problems are IRQ problems?  And what is
the right way to tell the system about those IRQs used by the ISA
cards?  The board has only an ARC Bios I and can not find any way to
define the ISA IRQs.

