From what I've read, SMP support is a bit different from 'normal' H/W
support.  Normal support for the foo2.2 card can be compiled in and
if the card's not there kernel execution continues.  I believe the SMP
support REALLY cares about what it finds.  An SMP kernel may work on
a uni system that *could* be SMP, but the AS200 ain't it.  He's going
to be looking for the arbitration hardware that tells which processor
who owns the bus at any given time.

And if you think your kitchen is bad, you don't wanna see mine!
