On Mon, 21 Dec 1998, Paul Tomblin wrote:

> Quoting Stephen Williams (steve@icarus.com):
> > 
> > ptomblin@xcski.com said:
> > > I went from 32Mb to 96Mb in my UDB as well, without even a kernel
> > > recompile.
> > 
> > > But then again, I don't remember having to change anything in my Intel
> > > machine when it went from 32Mb to 64Mb either. 
> > 
> > 2.0.35 or earlier (i think I got the version right) had trouble detecting
> > memory above 64Meg on ix86 systems only because of icky BIOS issues. The
> > alpha systems don't have these problems, and the very latest ix86 kernels
> > have been (from what I hear) cured at well.
> 
> Now that you mention it, I fixed that with an option in LILO in my machine at
> work.  4 of us at work had been using Linux for a couple of months before I
> realized that I only had 64Mb available, instead of the 128Mb I got when I
> used NT.  I fixed my lilo.conf, and I was happy - and I told the others about
> it, and they were happy too.

Yep, but the point is that with x86 kernels >= 2.0.36, there is no
need to use the lilo 'fix'. The kernel will detect the correct amount
of memory automatically. If you were to change the amount of memory
(up or down) on those systems, you'd have to remember to change the
lilo settings. If you added memory and forgot to change 'mem=', you'd
be in the same situation you described above (your system wouldn't be
using all its available memory). If you removed memory and forgot to
change the 'mem=' setting, the system would most likely not boot.

One can still use the lilo 'mem=xx' feature for testing, for example,
low memory conditions [ie. setting mem=8M to see if the vm subsystem
breaks when running X, quake2, netscape, xemacs, a kernel compile, and
an egcs compile simulutaneously ;-> ].

