In message <5518FE2C95E6D111B1A500104B72113B02042184@alfexc8.alf.dec.com>, Bob 
Segrest writes:
> Hello Joe,
> 
> I am using RedHat Version 5.2 and have observed the same symptoms on both
> Intel and Alpha systems in my lab.
> 
> I am using xdm and logging in after X starts up...
> 
> I have tried creating a variety of .xinitrc, .xsession and .fvwmrc files in
> my $HOME directory with no apparent effect.  I have been through a lot of
> man pages, how to files and mail archives during the past few days.  I have
> also grep and mored my way through a lot of start up scripts without finding
> the bit that starts up the default fvwm applications.  It does not appear to
> happen the system.fvwmrc file as has been suggested by others...

Red Hat has moved some of X11's files/directories. It has been my experience
that *on some* files/directories critical links are not made. Have you
checked /usr/X11R6/lib/X11? I found a directory there (fvwm2) that
should have been a link back to /etc/X11.

As I see you are using xdm, the line of execution is as follows (from
the login):

1. GiveConsole
2. Xsetup_0
3. Xsession

You will see that Xsession executes ~/.xsession which is linked to
~/.Xclients. This is where your window manager *should* normally be
executed (the *last* item).

I hope this helps. I am fairly certain you have a missing link or else
your configuration is totally screwed up (not necessarily your fault).

Bob

