>> changing your HOSTNAME...
>Thanks for all of the ideas. I wrote a script and when I did tail -f
>/etc/HOSTNAME I got:
>
>X(719): unaligned trap at ooooooo12022d8a4: 00000155564f7244 2d 8 (and 
3
>more)
>then the change came.

Uh.. hmm... okay... it seems like your X is giving some problems, but I 
doubt that is directly related to your HOSTNAME concerns.


>
>ps -aux showed pid 719 to be /usr/X11R6/bin/Xwrapper owned by root. (I
>am dialing in from a user not root)

This I don't think has much to do with it... in fact, X should not have 
anything to do with the networking.... You may have just discovered 
another problem you may have... but unalignment errors are so common on 
Alpha... especially with 32bit ported code.... I've been trying to fix a 
dozen things in the last month or so....

>> By the way, what's the big deal? Is this changing HOSTNAME screwing 
up
>> some application that happens to use it?
>
>It happens both with usernet from redhat and my own script. If this is
>not a big problem I certainly can live with it. Not much of a security
>problem as I don't log in often or for long periods. Rarely more than 
15
>to 20 mins at a time.

I don't think a changing HOSTNAME is anything of a security problem. In 
some sense, it is perhaps more secure, as a potential intruder can't 
lock down on a target if you hostname/IP changes all the time.

